Our copy of FMDB must not answer to the application's name.

A class name is registered by the ObjC runtime for every loaded image, and two images that register
the same name make the runtime pick ONE OF THEM ARBITRARILY. It says so:

  objc[2]: Class FMDatabaseQueue is implemented in both
    /System/Library/PrivateFrameworks/FMDB.framework/Versions/A/FMDB and
    /Applications/iA Writer.app/Contents/Frameworks/Kit.framework/Versions/A/Kit.
    One of the two will be used. Which one is undefined.

iA Writer carries its own FMDB inside Kit.framework and runs its library indexing through it, so
when our copy won the toss its queries were served by a different build of FMDB than the one it was
compiled against. macOS ships this framework too and does not collide, because a system copy of
third party code is renamed before it ships.

LaunchServices is the only thing here that uses FMDB and it includes these headers, so it follows
the rename automatically. The two generated header maps list the new file so it reaches the SDK.

